      <memberdef kind="variable" id="structswim__member_1a59e3542d0bb5dcbfa01c5c1897481654" prot="public" static="no" mutable="no">
        <type>bool</type>
        <definition>bool swim_member::is_payload_up_to_date</definition>
        <argsstring></argsstring>
        <name>is_payload_up_to_date</name>
        <briefdescription>
<para>True, if the payload is thought to be of the most actual version. </para>        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>In such a case it can be disseminated further. Otherwise <emphasis>payload</emphasis> is suspected to be outdated and can be updated in two cases only:</para><para>1) when it is received with a bigger incarnation from anywhere;</para><para>2) when it is received with the same incarnation, but local payload is outdated.</para><para>A payload can become outdated, if anyhow a new incarnation of the member has been learned, but not a new payload. For example, a message with new payload could be lost, and at the same time this instance responds to a ping with newly incarnated ack. The ack receiver will learn the new incarnation, but not the new payload.</para><para>In this case it can&apos;t be said exactly whether the member has updated payload, or another attribute. The only way here is to wait until the most actual payload will be received from another instance. Note, that such an instance always exists - the payload originator instance. </para>        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
        <location file="/home/tsafin/tarantool/src/lib/swim/swim.c" line="362" column="1" bodyfile="/home/tsafin/tarantool/src/lib/swim/swim.c" bodystart="362" bodyend="-1"/>
      </memberdef>
